.TH CSTRING_RFIND 3 "2026-09-28" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
cstring_rfind \- find the last occurrence of a substring
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cstring.h>

size_t cstring_rfind(cstr s, const char *needle);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
This function looks for the last occurrence of needle in the string and
returns the zero-based index where it starts. Overlapping occurrences
count, so the last "aba" in "ababa" starts at index 2.

For a string of 256 bytes or more the search goes from the end of the
string toward its start and stops at the first match it meets, so it
never reads the bytes to the left of that match. Its time is then linear
in the length of the string plus the length of needle, whatever the two
hold. A shorter string is searched from its start, because at that
length that is faster.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
s        String to search
needle   Substring to find
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
Zero-based index of the last occurrence
cstring_length(s) if needle is an empty string (the past-the-end position)
ccol_invalid_size ((size_t)-1) if needle is not found, or if needle is NULL
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
Asserts if s is NULL
A needle of up to 128 bytes needs no memory. With a longer needle the
search can take one temporary table (one size_t per byte of needle) from
the allocator of s, and it frees that table before the call returns. If
the allocator refuses the table the answer is the same, but the time can
then grow with the product of the two lengths
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cstring_find (3)
